e written as𝑧𝜇 (𝑥) = 𝑧𝜇 (𝑥) + 𝜖𝑧] (𝑥) 𝑧𝜇,] + 𝑂 (𝜖2) . (36)\n\nSubstituting from (36) into (30), we get𝑥𝜇 = 𝑥𝜇 + 𝜖𝑧𝜇 (𝑥) + 𝑂 (𝜖2) . (37)\n\nConsequently, we can write𝜕𝑥𝜇𝜕𝑥] = 𝛿𝜇] − 𝜖𝑧𝜇,],𝜕𝑥𝜇𝜕𝑥] = 𝛿𝜇] + 𝜖𝑧𝜇,] + 𝑂 (𝜖2) .\n(38)\n\nThen, the Jacobian expressions of the above matrices can be\ngiven as 𝜕𝑥𝜕𝑥 = 1 − 𝜖𝑧𝛼,𝛼 + 𝑂 (𝜖2) ,𝜕𝑥𝜕𝑥 = 1 + 𝜖𝑧𝛼,𝛼 + 𝑂 (𝜖2) ,\n\n(39)\n\nsince the difference between 𝑧𝜇(𝑥) and 𝑧𝜇(𝑥) would be of\norder 𝜖2 as shown by (36).\n\nNow, we express 𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) in terms of 𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) in two different\n\nways:\n(i) Using the vector transformation law:\n\n𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) = 𝜕𝑥]𝜕𝑥𝜇 𝜆]𝑖 (𝑥)= 𝛿]𝜇𝜆]\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) + 𝜖𝜆]\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) 𝑧] ,𝜇 + 𝑂 (𝜖2)\n= 𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) + 𝜖𝜆]\n𝑖\n\n𝑧] ,𝜇 + 𝑂 (𝜖2)\n(40)\n\n(ii) Using Taylor expansion:𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) = 𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥 − 𝜖𝑧) = 𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) − 𝜖𝜆𝜇,]\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) 𝑧] + 𝑂 (𝜖2)\n= 𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) − 𝜖𝜆𝜇,]\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) 𝑧] + 𝑂 (𝜖2) (41)\n\nWehave dropped the bar in the second termon the right hand\nside of this equation as the difference would be of order 𝜖2.\nComparing (40) and (41) and using (22), we get𝜆𝜇\n\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) − 𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) = 𝜖ℎ𝜇\n𝑖\n\n+ 𝑂 (𝜖2) , (42)\n\nwhere ℎ𝜇\n𝑖\n\n= 𝜆]\n𝑖\n\n𝑧] ,𝜇 + 𝜆𝜇,]\n𝑖\n\n𝑧] = 𝜆]\n𝑖\n\n𝑧]‖𝜇\n−\n\n+ 𝜆𝜇‖]\n+\n\n𝑖\n\n𝑧], (43)\n\nand the double stroke operator “‖” is defined by (18).\n\n\n\nAdvances in Mathematical Physics 5\n\nNow, we treat the Lagrangian density in the two different\nways mentioned above.\n\n(a) Using Taylor expansion,\n∗\n\nL (𝑥) = ∗\n\nL (𝑥 − 𝜖𝑧)\n= ∗\n\nL (𝑥) − 𝜖 ∗L (𝑥),𝛼 𝑧𝛼 + 𝑂 (𝜖2) ; (44)\n\nalso, we have dropped the bar in the second term on the right\nhand side of this equation as the difference would be of order𝜖2.\n\nThe Lagrangian density\n∗\n\nL(𝑥) in (44) means\n∗\n\nL (𝑥) ≡ ∗\n\nL(𝜆𝜇\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) , 𝜆𝜇,]\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥) , 𝜆𝜇,]𝜎\n𝑖\n\n(𝑥)) . (45)\n\n(b) Using scalar transformation,\n∗𝐿 (𝑥) = ∗𝐿 (𝑥) ,\n𝜆 ∗𝐿 (𝑥) = 𝜆 ∗𝐿 (𝑥) = 𝜆 ∗𝐿 (𝑥) 𝜕𝑥𝜕𝑥 ;\n\n(46)\n\nthen, using (20) and (39), we get\n∗\n\nL (𝑥) = ∗\n\nL (𝑥) + 𝜖 ∗L (𝑥) 𝑧𝛼,𝛼 + 𝑂 (𝜖2) . (47)\n\nComparing (44) and (47), we have\n∗\n\nL (𝑥) − ∗L (𝑥) = 𝜖